m('02-collision-theory','Collision theory and catalysts',p('Activation energy','Minimum energy for successful reaction.','Particles must collide and reach threshold.','Not every collision reacts.','More successes each second: higher rate.'),p('Concentration and pressure','More solution particles per unit volume.','Higher gas pressure packs particles closer.','Collision frequency increases.','Same temperature does not increase individual speed.'),p('Surface area','Smaller solid pieces at same mass.','Greater exposed area.','More particles accessible for collisions.','Not more total substance.'),p('Temperature','Particles gain kinetic energy and move faster.','Collisions more frequent and energetic.','More exceed activation energy.','Explain both effects.'),p('Catalytic pathway','Different pathway, lower activation energy.','Catalyst regenerated, not used up overall.','Suitable catalyst needed for reaction.','Enzymes act as biological catalysts.'),p('Profiles and yield','Lower peak but same reactant/product levels.','Overall energy change unchanged.','Both reversible directions faster.','Equilibrium sooner, same position/yield.')),
